this budget was supposed to be about  taking Britain forward overcame economic  waters instead it's gonna be all about  this the black hole in the chancellor's  accounts for the past eight years  Britain's public finances have looked a  little bit like this a vast vortex at  the heart of governments sucking money  out of its coffers now George Osborne  was rather hoping that this was going to  dry up and he'd be left with a healthy  balance sheet just why he devised  himself too hard fiscal rules the first  is that the national debt must fall  every year as a percentage of GDP the  second is the government needs to have a  surplus in other words to collect more  tax each year than it spends by 2019-20  but with the economy weakening the  boffins working here at the Treasury are  going to find it far more difficult to  meet those rules than back in the autumn  first the national debt might actually  still be rising his pledge to cut debt  as a share of our economy every year  going forwards and in principle there  are good reasons to want to do that but  in practice he's been aiming to do it  really judge driven because of his  selling off of assets and that's pretty  much cheating the spirit of that rule  and it now looks like some of those  asset sales won't go ahead and if that's  the case he might end up breaking the  actual practice of the rule as well as  the spirit of it second the surface rule  doesn't look quite so easy according to  the man who actually helped write it  until last year Rupert howson worked  here as the Chancellor's chief of staff  he's given himself a little bit of  wiggle room to start with albeit 10  billion pounds in five years time is not  that much in terms of what he can do to  achieve that you know there are no  there's no third way in a way the seds  either you're going to put up taxis or  you're going to cut spending and I think  that say you know in the budget and the  Autumn Statement since the election  there's been a bit of both I would  expect that to continue to understand  why consider what's happening here in  Sheffield for centuries this is where  the world came for steel today these  mills are under permanent threat in the  face of Chinese competition and  collapsing metal prices this is pretty  much smack bang in the middle of the  Chancellor's northern powerhouse halfway  between Sheffield and Manchester and  every way look there are reminders of  what used to make Britain great  got the steel mills the industrial  heartlands this reservoir was actually  used by the dam Busters for their  practice flights but for all of the talk  the economy here is still struggling and  wages are still stagnant weak wages mean  weak tax revenues falling share prices  mean falling capital gains tax and with  the EU referendum on the horizon the  Chancellor is desperate not to do  anything to alienate voters this week so  his hands are tied this was supposed to  be a bold budget about reshaping Britain  instead it's likely to be dominated by  worries about money still escaping down  the plughole Eddie Conway Sky News
 

